  <!-- Also on the table -->
  <div>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">Also on the Christmas Table</h2>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      Struffoli, roccocò and mustaccioli rarely come alone. A Neapolitan Christmas tray usually holds some of these too:
    </p>
    <ul class="space-y-3 mb-6">
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span><span class="text-slate-700"><strong>Susamielli</strong>: S-shaped biscuits of flour, honey, almonds and pisto, covered in sesame seeds.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span><span class="text-slate-700"><strong>Sapienze</strong>: a straight, elongated cousin of susamielli, found in Naples, Caserta and Sorrento.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span><span class="text-slate-700"><strong>Raffiuoli</strong>: discs of sponge cake with apricot jam under a white sugar glaze.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span><span class="text-slate-700"><strong>Divino amore</strong>: almond paste sweets with apricot jam and sugar glaze.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span><span class="text-slate-700"><strong>Pastiera</strong>: usually an Easter cake, but in Naples it shows up at Christmas too.</span></li>
      <li class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="w-2 h-2 bg-med-gold rounded-full mt-2.5 flex-shrink-0"></span><span class="text-slate-700"><strong>Torrone</strong>: nougat and chocolate torroncini, with their own <a href="/en-us/blog/torrone-recipe/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">torrone recipe</a> on this blog.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- Where to buy -->
  <div>
    <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">Where to Buy Them on the Coast and the Peninsula</h2>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      In December the pastry shops of Sorrento, the peninsula towns and the Amalfi Coast fill their counters with trays of struffoli and bags of roccocò and mustaccioli. In Amalfi and Minori, pastry shops we already mention on this site, such as Pansa in Amalfi and Sal De Riso in Minori, are a good place to start: ask at the counter what they have that week.
    </p>
    <p class="text-slate-600 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-6">
      If you want to take some home, choose roccocò and mustaccioli: they are dry, they keep, and they survive a suitcase. Struffoli are sticky with honey and best eaten on the spot. A December trip also lines up with the <a href="/en-us/blog/christmas-lights-sorrento-salerno/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">Christmas lights of Sorrento and Salerno</a>, so you can buy your sweets and see the lights on the same evening.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Expert Tips -->
  <div class="bg-white p-8 rounded-[2rem] shadow-xl border border-slate-100">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-6">
      <svg class="w-6 h-6 text-med-gold flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M9 18h6"/><path d="M10 22h4"/><path d="M15.09 14c.18-.98.65-1.74 1.41-2.5A4.65 4.65 0 0 0 18 8 6 6 0 0 0 6 8c0 1 .23 2.23 1.5 3.5A4.61 4.61 0 0 1 8.91 14"/></svg>
      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold">Expert Tips</h3>
    </div>
    <div class="space-y-6">
      <div>
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Sift off the flour before frying</p>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">Shake the cut struffoli in a sieve to remove all the loose flour, then let them rest 10 minutes on baking paper before they go into the oil.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Fry ahead, honey later</p>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">Plain fried struffoli keep well in a sealed bag. Coat them in warm honey on the day you serve them.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Underbake roccocò slightly</p>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">Take them out while they still seem too soft. They firm up as they cool, and every extra minute in the oven makes them harder.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-1">Glaze mustaccioli only when cold</p>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">Warm biscuits break and melt the chocolate unevenly. Wait until they are completely cool on the tray before dipping.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

